Phe 149,\nL767: \nL768: and the N6 atom of the base hydrogen bonds to the side chain of\nL769: Asn 112 (2.9 Å) from a symmetry-related molecule. When the\nL770: \nL771: xanthine is modeled to replace the adenine base of AMPPNP, the\nL772: \nL773: O2 atom of XTP, which is absent in adenine, would form a\nL774: \nL775: hydrogen bond with N d 2 of Asn 17, a strictly conserved residue,\nL776: \nL777: whose O d 1 group is hydrogen bonded to the backbone amide\nL778: \nL779: group of Asn 19. While the O2 atom of XTP is not present in ino-\nL780: \nL781: sine, the amino group is in the equivalent position of guanine.\nL782: \nL783: This may explain why GTP is a less efficient substrate compared\nL784: to XTP or ITP. Also the O6 atom of xanthine, inosine or guanine\nL785: \nL786: could form a hydrogen bond to the N d 2 of Asn 19 with slight\nL787: \nL788: conformational changes. However, it is possible that the binding\nL789: \nL790: of a substrate may induce local conformational changes in the\nL791: \nL792: active site, consistent with the observation that crystals were\nL793: \nL794: damaged upon addition of substrate or product.\nL795: \nL796: Implication of biological function\nL797: \nL798: Since no XTPase has been identified from any organism, the cellu-\nL799: lar role of Mj0226 protein is difficult to predict. However, the pos-\nL800: \nL801: sible biochemical function of the enzyme deduced from our\nL802: \nL803: structural studies provides clues for understanding its cellular role.\nL804: \nL805: Fig 4 a,Thin layer chromatography showing the pyrophosphate releasing\nL806: \nL807: NTPase activities of Mj0226 with GTP. Lane 1: control GTP; 2: GTP with\nL808: \nL809: Mj0226 (0.3 nM); 3: GTP with Mj0226 (3 nM); 4: GTP with Mj0226 (30 nM); 5:\nL810: \nL811: GTP with Mj0226 (30 nM) and EDTA (10 mM); 6: GTP with BSA (10 m g).\nL812: b,HPLC chromatograms showing XTPase activities of Mj0226. Top: XTP in\nL813: \nL814: the absence of Mj0226; middle: XTP incubated with Mj0226 and 5 mM\nL815: \nL816: MgCl 2; bottom: XTP incubated with only Mj0226. c,HPLC chromatograms\nL817: \nL818: showing XDPase activities of Mj0226. Top: XDP in the absence of Mj0226:\nL819: \nL820: bottom: XDP incubated with Mj0226 and 5 mM MgCl 2.\nL821: \nL822: a b\nL823: \nL824: c\nL825: \nL826: Table 2 Kinetic parameters of Mj0226 with various nucleotides\nL827: \nL828: K\nL829: \nL830: cat\nL831: \nL832: (s\nL833: \nL834: -1\nL835: \nL836: )K\nL837: \nL838: m\nL839: \nL840: (mM) K\nL841: \nL842: cat\nL843: \nL844: / K\nL845: \nL846: m\nL847: \nL848: XTP 1009.37 0.10 10195.66\nL849: \nL850: ITP 911.72 0.15 5998.16\nL851: GTP 97.65 1.11 87.66\nL852: \nL853: dGTP 96.64 1.13 85.52\nL854: \nL855: ATP 1.02 7.04 0.15\nL856: \nL857: CTP 2.23 1.45 1.54\nL858: \nL859: TTP 1.77 0.30 5.90\nL860: \nL861: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL862: \nL863: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL864: \nL865: articles\nL866: \nL867: Recently, it has been observed in Saccharomyces cerevisiae\nL868: \nL869: that inactivation of a gene (gene I.D. P47119) resulted in\nL870: \nL871: increased sensitivity of yeast to the base analog 6-N-hydroxy-\nL872: \nL873: aminopurine (HAP). In addition, adenine-requiring haploid\nL874: strains are unable to grow on HAP as the sole adenine source,\nL875: \nL876: presumably because the conversion of HAP to HAP triphosphate\nL877: \nL878: and subsequent incorporation into DNA is lethal\nL879: \nL880: 33–36\nL881: \nL882: . This yeast\nL883: \nL884: protein is homologous (about 30 % sequence identity) to Mj0226\nL885: \nL886: protein (Fig. 5). Our structural and biochemical information, in\nL887: \nL888: conjunction with the moderate sequence similarity, suggest that\nL889: \nL890: nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999 695\nL891: the cellular function of Mj0226 protein may be to prevent muta-\nL892: \nL893: tions by protecting DNA from the incorporation of modified\nL894: \nL895: purine bases such as dXTP or dITP. Alternatively, the yeast pro-\nL896: \nL897: tein may be a HAPTP/dHAPTP triphosphatase.\nL898: \nL899: Although we have not tested the NTPase activities of Mj0226\nL900: \nL901: protein on dITP and dXTP (not available commercially), both\nL902: \nL903: substrates are likely to be hydrolyzed to a similar degree as ITP\nL904: \nL905: and XTP, respectively, since there is no strong interaction at the 2'\nL906: oxygen of the sugar ring in the structure, and both dGTP and\nL907: \nL908: GTP show similar kinetic parameters. An alternative possible cel-\nL909: \nL910: lular function of Mj0226 protein can be inferred from\nL911: \nL912: the observation that GTP, ITP or XTP act as different\nL913: \nL914: signal amplifiers and signal sorters of G-proteins\nL915: \nL916: 37\nL917: \nL918: .\nL919: \nL920: In summary, we have demonstrated that the three-\nL921: \nL922: dimensional structure of a hypothetical protein (with\nL923: \nL924: no sequence homologs of known functions) may\nL925: \nL926: enable us to infer its biochemical functions, which\nL927: then can be experimentally tested. This is the premise\nL928: \nL929: of structural genomics\nL930: \nL931: 13–16\nL932: \nL933: .\nL934: \nL935: Methods\nL936: \nL937: The Mj0226 gene was subcloned into PET3a vector,\nL938: \nL939: expressed in E. coli BL21, and sequenced to ensure that\nL940: \nL941: there were no mutations introduced during the cloning\nL942: \nL943: process. The recombinant protein was purified to near\nL944: \nL945: homogeneity by heat-denaturation of E. coli proteins,\nL946: \nL947: followed by anion exchange and gel filtration. The crys-\nL948: \nL949: tallization conditions were screened by the sparse-\nL950: \nL951: matrix method\nL952: \nL953: 38\nL954: (Hampton Research Co.). The crystals\nL955: \nL956: for data collection were grown by hanging-drop vapor\nL957: \nL958: diffusion method. The aqueous protein solution (10 mg\nL959: \nL960: ml\nL961: \nL962: -1\nL963: \nL964: ) in 20 mM potassium phosphate, pH 7.5, 20 mM\nL965: \nL966: NaCl, was mixed with an equal volume of reservoir\nL967: \nL968: buffer containing 0.1 M N-(2-acetamido)-iminodiacetic\nL969: \nL970: acid, pH 6.5, 10% PEG 6K, and 20% isopropanol, and the\nL971: \nL972: mixture was equilibrated with the reservoir buffer\nL973: \nL974: through vapor phase. Crystals of the size 0.2 ´0.3 ´0.5\nL975: mm were formed after a week. Space group of the crys-\nL976: \nL977: tals belongs to P2\nL978: \nL979: 1\nL980: \nL981: 2\nL982: \nL983: 1\nL984: \nL985: 2\nL986: \nL987: 1\nL988: \nL989: with unit cell dimensions of a =\nL990: \nL991: 44.4 Å, b = 72.9 Å and c = 141.4 Å. The crystal contained\nL992: \nL993: two molecules per asymmetric unit and diffracted to\nL994: \nL995: beyond 2.2 Å resolution. The native and derivative data\nL996: \nL997: sets were collected using a MAR research image plate\nL998: \nL999: mounted on a RIGAKU RU-200 generator. The structure\nL1000: \nL1001: of Mj0226 protein was determined by the MIR method,\nL1002: Fig. 5 Sequence alignment of Mj0226 with other homologous proteins. Secondary structures of Mj0226 are shown. The conserved residues from 17\nL1003: \nL1004: different proteins are boxed (yellow) and only 7 proteins are shown for clarity.\nL1005: \nL1006: Fig. 6 a,A |F o| - |F c| map showing the AMPPNP. The map was contoured at 3 s. b,A\nL1007: \nL1008: stereodiagram showing the nucleotide binding region within Mj0226 protein. Each\nL1009: \nL1010: hydrogen bond between the ligand (yellow) and residues (blue) are shown.\nL1011: \nL1012: a\nL1013: \nL1014: b\nL1015: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL1016: \nL1017: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL1018: \nL1019: articles\nL1020: \nL1021: and the statistics of the crystallographic analysis are presented in\nL1022: \nL1023: Table 1. The MIR phases were calculated using four heavy-atom\nL1024: \nL1025: derivatives (Thimerosal, PCMB, KAuCl\nL1026: \nL1027: 4\nL1028: \nL1029: , KAu(CN)\nL1030: \nL1031: 4\nL1032: \nL1033: ), and the phases\nL1034: \nL1035: had a mean figure of merit of 0.55 at 12–3.0 Å resolution. Solvent\nL1036: \nL1037: flipping and density averaging using the program Solomon\nL1038: \nL1039: 39\nL1040: \nL1041: signifi-\nL1042: cantly improved the initial MIR maps, showing clear chain trace in\nL1043: \nL1044: most regions except the first nine residues at the N-terminus. The\nL1045: \nL1046: structural model was built using CHAIN\nL1047: \nL1048: 40\nL1049: \nL1050: and refined using X-PLOR\nL1051: \nL1052: 41\nL1053: \nL1054: .\nL1055: \nL1056: The final crystallographic R-factor of 18.9 % and R\nL1057: \nL1058: free\nL1059: \nL1060: of 26.7% for\nL1061: \nL1062: 6–2.2 Å data (20,346 reflections with |F| > 2 s; r.m.s. deviation in bond\nL1063: \nL1064: length = 0.011 Å, and in-bond angles = 1.45°). The final model con-\nL1065: \nL1066: tains 2,990 protein atoms and 280 water molecules. The first nine\nL1067: residues are not visible in the density map and not included in the\nL1068: \nL1069: model. For the structure of the complex between Mj0226 protein\nL1070: \nL1071: and AMPPNP, the final crystallographic R-factor is 20.0% and R\nL1072: \nL1073: free\nL1074: \nL1075: is\nL1076: \nL1077: 25.3% for 8–2.2 Å data (21,998 reflections with |F| > 2 s. The r.m.s.\nL1078: \nL1079: deviation in bond length is 0.012 Å, and that in bond angles is 1.6°.\nL1080: \nL1081: The final model contains 3,021 protein atoms and 161 water mole-\nL1082: \nL1083: cules.\nL1084: \nL1085: Nucleotide binding assays. The binding assays for ATP or GTP\nL1086: were carried out by incubating various amounts of [g-\nL1087: \nL1088: 32\nL1089: \nL1090: P]ATP or\nL1091: \nL1092: [g-\nL1093: \nL1094: 32\nL1095: \nL1096: P]GTP in 20 ul mixture containing 25 mM HEPES, pH 7.6, 4 mM\nL1097: \nL1098: MgCl\nL1099: \nL1100: 2\nL1101: \nL1102: , 0.3\nL1103: \nL1104: –\nL1105: \nL1106: 30 nM of Mj0226 protein at 37°C. Aliquots were ana-\nL1107: \nL1108: lyzed by native PAGE and thin-layer chromatography followed by\nL1109: \nL1110: autoradiography.\nL1111: \nL1112: NTPase activity assay. The NTPase activity of Mj0226 protein on\nL1113: \nL1114: several nucleotides was measured at 80 °C in a 40 m l reaction mix-\nL1115: \nL1116: 696 nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999\nL1117: ture containing 25 mM HEPES pH 7.6, 4 mM MgCl\nL1118: \nL1119: 2\nL1120: \nL1121: and 6.23 nmol of\nL1122: \nL1123: the purified protein. At the selected time points, the reaction was\nL1124: \nL1125: stopped by the addition of trichloroacetic acid at final concentra-\nL1126: \nL1127: tion of 0.1%. Reaction mixtures were analyzed on a Hypersil SAX\nL1128: \nL1129: column, by use of a HPLC system equipped with a recording integra-\nL1130: \nL1131: tor. The nucleotides were bound to the column in water and eluted,\nL1132: \nL1133: at a flow rate of 1 ml min\nL1134: \nL1135: -1\nL1136: \nL1137: , with a linear gradient from 5 mM\nL1138: ammonium phosphate (pH 2.8) to 750 mM of ammonium phos-\nL1139: \nL1140: phate (pH 3.7). The kinetic parameters were calculated using pro-\nL1141: \nL1142: gram ENZFITTER (Biosoft Co.).\nL1143: \nL1144: Coordinates. Coordinates of apo Mj0226 and nucleotide bound\nL1145: \nL1146: Mj0226 have been deposited in the Protein Data Bank (accession\nL1147: \nL1148: codes 1b78 and 2mjp, respectively).\nL1149: \nL1150: Acknowledgments\nL1151: \nL1152: We are grateful to C.A. Caperelli (University of Cincinnati) for providing\nL1153: \nL1154: glycinamide ribonucleotide and 10-formyl-5,8-dideazafolate, and helpful\nL1155: comments on the GAR assay. We also thank B.K. Lee and J.S. Kim for the\nL1156: \nL1157: program SHEBA. This work was supported by funds from the Korea Institute of\nL1158: \nL1159: Science and Technology (KIST 2000 program), Korean Ministry of Science and\nL1160: \nL1161: Technology (Biotech 2000 program), Korean Ministry of Health and Welfare,\nL1162: \nL1163: Office of 